WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.180
The first film kept allele tiles separate while the phenotype changed.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.440
How can we follow those copies when people cannot be crossed for an experiment?

3
00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:15.140
We must use evidence that already exists, then match each question to the right

4
00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:18.920
scale. Each view can answer only some questions.

5
00:00:22.517 --> 00:00:25.077
A pedigree turns a family into a model.

6
00:00:26.497 --> 00:00:30.357
Squares and circles stand for people, and shading marks the chosen trait.

7
00:00:32.057 --> 00:00:33.557
Lines connect partners and children.

8
00:00:35.537 --> 00:00:38.777
Generations stack downward, letting a pattern unfold through time.

9
00:00:40.037 --> 00:00:42.137
The shape is a symbol, not a genotype.

10
00:00:43.217 --> 00:00:46.317
The parents do not show the trait, yet their child does.

11
00:00:47.617 --> 00:00:51.397
Which simple model fits this clue better: dominant or recessive?

12
00:00:52.557 --> 00:00:56.137
Hold both possibilities beside the family before you decide.

13
00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:03.970
A recessive model can fit.

14
00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:10.130
Each unshaded parent may carry a recessive allele, and the child may receive both

15
00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:16.210
copies. This pattern points toward recessive inheritance, but a small family does

16
00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:20.370
not prove it. The parents are carriers in this simple model.

17
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.820
Geneticists test a model against every person, not a striking branch.

18
00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:32.640
A dominant trait usually appears in each generation, and a shaded child usually has

19
00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.820
a shaded parent. A new DNA change can be an exception.

20
00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:40.940
A small family can still fit several explanations.

21
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:46.740
Alleles remain separate, and each genetic tool reveals a different scale.

22
00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:50.020
A pedigree works at the family scale.

23
00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:55.840
It can test inheritance models, but it cannot show the allele copies directly.

24
00:01:57.033 --> 00:02:00.033
Now zoom from a person into a cell with a nucleus.

25
00:02:01.393 --> 00:02:06.073
A karyotype sorts photographed chromosomes into matched pairs by size and shape.

26
00:02:07.253 --> 00:02:10.933
It answers questions about whole chromosomes and large changes.

27
00:02:12.493 --> 00:02:15.373
Matched partners make an extra or missing copy visible.

28
00:02:16.483 --> 00:02:19.443
An extra chromosome is large enough to count.

29
00:02:20.703 --> 00:02:25.383
A single gene, such as the ABO gene, is far too small to identify here.

30
00:02:26.503 --> 00:02:30.983
The picture can reveal scale-sized changes, not every inherited detail.

31
00:02:32.343 --> 00:02:35.603
A karyotype cannot identify a person's ABO alleles.

32
00:02:36.583 --> 00:02:41.403
Nearly every body cell with a nucleus carries the same DNA and genes.

33
00:02:42.923 --> 00:02:46.423
Skin and muscle cells differ because they use different gene sets.

34
00:02:47.823 --> 00:02:52.303
Mature red blood cells lose their nuclei, leaving no chromosomes to count.

35
00:02:53.333 --> 00:02:55.753
Start with the question, then choose the view.

36
00:02:57.073 --> 00:03:02.673
Use a cross for possible allele combinations, a pedigree for family patterns, and a

37
00:03:02.673 --> 00:03:04.173
karyotype for whole chromosomes.

38
00:03:05.673 --> 00:03:08.153
No view can answer every genetics question.

39
00:03:09.393 --> 00:03:11.293
Match the tool to the scale of evidence.
